   "conclusion": {
    "diagnosis": "Jobs 1, 2, and 4 genuinely failed inspection while job 3 passed. All four jobs ran through the identical CNC-01 → CNC-02 → CNC-03 → CNC-04 → CNC-05 sequence before inspection (E5, E6, E7, E9), and job 3 passed through CNC-05 (ticks 396–470) immediately after job 2 failed there (tick 396) and before job 4 failed (tick 547), so a persistent CNC-05 fault is ruled out. CNC-05 vibration and following_error are elevated but steady across ticks 240–550 with no spike at the failure ticks (E10, E11, E12). The pattern is most consistent with a job-specific production difference (material lot, parameters, operator, or shift) — H1 — rather than an inspection malfunction (job 3 passed between two failures) or one broken machine. Confidence is about 0.70, below the 0.90 threshold for intervene, and none of the available actions (replace_bearing, reset_setting, replace_servo_drive, repair_hydraulics) addresses a material/lot/parameter cause. Evidence that would raise confidence: job parameter/lot records comparing job 3 to jobs 1, 2, 4, and sensor trends for CNC-01 through CNC-04 during the relevant job windows.",
